## ABSTRACT
Developing efficient catalysts for organic pollutants degradation is crucial for remediating the current severe water environment, yet  remains  a  great  challenge.  Herein,  we  report  silver  nanoparticles  immobilized  on  an  amine-functionalized  metal-organic framework (MOFs) $(\mathsf{A g/U i O{-}}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}})$ ) as a robust catalyst for the reduction of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP). The fabricated ${\mathsf{A g/U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{66}}{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ catalyst  exhibits  the  merits  of  superior  activities  (high  turnover  frequency  (TOF) $3.2\times10^{4}~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ and $k$ value $6.9\times10^{-2}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1};$ ,  costeffectiveness  under  the  lowest $N a B H_{4}$ concentration $(n_{\tt I N a B H}/n_{\tt I4-N P]}$ ,  200),  outstanding  cyclability  (10  recycling  runs),  and observable  long-term  durability,  significantly  outperforming  previously  reported  catalytic  system.  The  excellent  degradation efficiency  is  ascribed  to  the  favorable  microenvironment  modulation  of  unique  MOF  structure,  which  regulates  the  intrinsic properties  of  active  sites  and  improves  the  electron-transfer  process.  Notably,  the $A g/\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}N H_{2}$ also  promotes  the  catalytic degradation of several organic pollutants at room temperature and hence could find a broad application for water remediation. This  work  offers  a  new  avenue  for  the  development  of  high-performance  MOF-based  catalysts  with  excellent  activity  and durability.
## KEYWORDS
${\sf A g/U i O-}66{\sf-N H}_{2}$ , catalytic degradation, organic pollutants, 4-nitrophenol, water remediation
## Introduction
Nitroaromatic  compounds  released  in  the  environment  are notorious for being harmful and difficult to be degraded [1, 2]. On the contrary, functionalized anilines, the corresponding reduction products  of  these  organic  pollutants,  are  valuable  intermediates and  can  be  used  for  the  production  of  pesticides,  dyes,  and pharmaceuticals [3, 4]. Catalytic hydrogenation of nitro-aromatic compounds into novel high value-added organic compounds has been  regarded  as  one  of  the  most  effective  ways  [5, 6].  For instance,  4-nitrophenol  (4-NP),  which  is  highly  toxic  and carcinogenic, has long been considered as a "priority pollutant" [3]. However, the corresponding reduction product of 4-aminophenol (4-AP)  displays  the  great  value  as  a  significant  material  in  the synthesis  of  paracetamol  [1, 7–9].  This  conversion  process  is usually assisted with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ as a reductant. From the perspective of  reduction  potential  (4-NP/4-AP, $-0.76\mathrm{~V~}$ and $\mathrm{H_{3}B O_{3}/B H_{4}^{-}}$ , –1.33  V),  the  reaction  is  thermodynamically  feasible  [10]. However, this reaction must be assisted by a catalyst due to the presence of a kinetic hindrance. Remarkably, metal nanoparticles as high-performance catalysts have attracted attention due to their small size and more accessible active sites [11–15]. Among them, Ag nanoparticles are highly preferred because of the extraordinary electronic  properties  and  low  cost  compared  to  other  precious metals [16–18]. Nonetheless, subject to the limitation of particle distribution and loading capacity, the utilization efficiency of $\mathrm{Ag}$ still  appears  to  be  unsatisfactory.  Therefore,  it  is  necessary  to synthesize well-dispersed catalysts that require tunable interactions between the supports and active sites [18].
The  prior  reports  have  overlooked  the  importance  of  the supports,  which,  in  fact,  play  a  central  role  in  creating  the microenvironment around metal nanoparticles in addition to their intended  role  (as  anchors  for  dispersed  metal  nanoparticles) [19–21].  In  this  field,  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  have become  promising  decorating  supports  due  to  the  tunable structures, high internal surface areas, and uniform pores [22, 23]. Diversified  nodes  and  ligands  endow  supports  with  abundant coordination  sites  that  benefit  MOFs  as  ideal  hosts  for  metal nanoparticles [24]. Plenty of successful cases employing MOFs as the substrates to fabricate metal-based nanocomposites at a high temperature have been reported [25, 26]. However, the destroyed MOFs after pyrolysis lost their inherent advantages, thus resulting in a poor interaction between metal nanoparticles and substrates. Notably, the uncoordinated amine groups $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ located at the linkers as the Lewis base (provide lone pair electrons) in aminefunctionalized MOFs could efficiently stabilize the transition metal precursors  (provide  empty  d-orbitals)  [27, 28].  Moreover,  the various  functional  groups  dangling  on  the  linkers  could  further prevent  metal  nanoparticles  from  aggregation  via  modulating chemical environment of MOFs pore walls, producing enhanced catalysis  [29–31].  Accordingly,  it  is  urgent  and  necessary  to develop novel catalysts with more accessible metal active sites and favorable microenvironment modulation to enhance stability for liquid-phase catalytic reactions.
Herein, we have developed a novel approach for the fabrication of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst  by  utilizing  ammine-containing $\mathrm{[Ag(NH}_{3})_{2}]^{+}$ complex as a precursor to be immobilized on UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ where  the  free $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups  are  used  to  regulate  the microenvironment  around $\mathrm{Ag}$ sites.  The  obtained  Ag/UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalysts exhibit superior activities (high turnover frequency (TOF) value up to $3.2\times10^{4}~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ , which is 2.3 times that of the control $\mathrm{Ag/UiO-}66$ (TOF  value $1.4\times10^{4}~\mathrm{h}^{-1};$ ).  Impressively,  the results show that both TOF and $k\left(6.9\times10^{-2}{{s}^{-1}}\right)$ values of Ag/UiO66-NH2 under  the  lowest  NaBH4 concentration  (n[NaBH4]/n[4-NP], 200)  outperform  the  capacity  of  many  previously  reported catalysts. Notably, $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ displays stable cyclability over 10  times  and  excellent  durability  over  continuous-flow hydrogenation  reactions.  Mechanistic  studies  indicate  that  the favorable  microenvironment  modulation  regulates  the  intrinsic properties  of  active  sites,  thus  improving  the  electron-transfer process. Notably, this versatile $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ also promotes the catalytic  degradation  of  several  organic  pollutants  at  room temperature and hence could find a broad application for water remediation.  We  envision  that  this  work  will  pave  the  way  for rationally  designing  MOF-support  catalysts  with  excellent performance in the sustainable treatment of wastewater pollutants.
## 2    Experimental section
## 2.1    Synthesis of UiO-66 and UiO- ${\bf66-N H}_{2}$
The  UiO-66  and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were  prepared  according  to  the reported method with a minor modification [32]. Typically, $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(52~\mathrm{mg},~0.22~\mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}B D C/H_{2}B D C\mathrm{-NH_{2}}}$ $(36~\mathrm{mg/40~mg},$ $0.22\mathrm{\mmol}$ )  were  dissolved  in $44~\mathrm{mL}$ N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{7}{\mathrm{NO}}){\mathrm{\Omega}}$ containing 6 mL acetic acid (HAc, $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}O_{2}})$ . The resulting mixture was first sonicated for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently, after centrifugation and washing with  the  mixture  of  methanol $\mathrm{(CH_{4}O)}$ and  DMF,  the  obtained white/yellow precipitate was dried in vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.2    Synthesis of Ag/UiO-66 and $\mathbf{Ag/UiO-66-NH}_{2}$
Typically, $85~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was first dispersed in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of fresh $\mathrm{Ag(NH_{3})_{2}N O_{3}}$ solution $(4\mathrm{mM})$ , and followed by a reduction of $10~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ aqueous  solution $(3~\mathrm{mM})$ under  dark.  After centrifugation  and  washing  with  deionized  (DI)  water  for  three times,  the  obtained  black $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst  was  vacuum dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Ag/UiO-66 catalyst was prepared by the same method using the support of UiO-66.
## 2.3    Catalytic performance tests of $\mathbf{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}$
The procedure for batch catalytic reduction of 4-NP and organic dyes over $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is described as shown below.
Catalytic activities of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ assisted with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ as a reductant  were  evaluated  for  degradation  of  six  model  organic pollutants  (4-NP,  Rhodamine  B  (RhB),  Rhodamine  6G  (R6G), methylene blue (MB), Congo red (CR), and methyl orange (MO)) at 298 K. Typically, $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of 4-NP $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{'10~mM}}$ was first mixed with $15~\mathrm{mL}$ freshly prepared $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ $(2.0\ \mathrm{M})$ aqueous solution. Then $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of a dispersive catalyst aqueous mixture $(2\mathrm{\mg{mL^{-1}}},$ ) was added  into  the  yellow  solution  under  a  magnetic  stirring  (800 rpm) at 298 K. The conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP was measured by ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrophotometer after the filtration through  a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane  filter.  In  the  recyclability  test,  all reactions  were  carried  out  according  to  the  procedures  above. After  each  run,  the  used  catalyst  was  separated  by  vacuum filtration, washed with fresh $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4},$ and tested directly in the next run.  In  the  leaching  test,  the  catalyst  was  separated  by  vacuum filtration at the reaction of $30\ {\mathfrak{s}},$ and the filtrate was measured by UV−vis spectrophotometer to determine the conversation of 4-NP to 4-AP.
The procedure for microflow catalytic reduction of 4-NP over $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is described as shown below.
Before  the  reaction, $30~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalysts  were loaded  into  a  glass  column $(5\ \mathrm{mm}\times50\ \mathrm{mm})$ )  (Fig. S19  in  the ESM). Then, an aqueous mixture of 4-NP $(50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}) $ ) and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ $(200~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}})$ was added into the column with a peristaltic pump (BT101L, Lead Fluid, China) at different flow rates (0.5, 1.5, and $2.5~\mathrm{{mL}\cdot\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ ) at $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ The resulting solution was collected and analysed by a UV–vis spectrophotometer.
## 3    Results and discussion
The successful preparation of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ was verified by various characterization methods. Figure 2(a) shows representative SEM images for the as-prepared $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ which kept well-defined octahedral morphology as compared to pure UiO-66 or UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S4 in the ESM). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ indicated that small Ag nanoparticles were uniformly and highly dispersed on the MOF supports (Fig. 2(b)), which was further confirmed by the  elemental  mapping  images  (Fig. S5  in  the  ESM).  The $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles possessed an average diameter of $8.8\pm0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ in $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ which is smaller than that of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO-}66$ ( $\cdot13.2\pm$ $0.2~\mathrm{nm}$ , Fig. S6 in the ESM). This result can be ascribed that the $\boldsymbol{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups  could  stabilize  the $\mathrm{[Ag(NH_{3})_{2}]N O_{3}}$ precursor  and further  prevent  their  aggregations  during  the  reduction  process.
High-resolution  TEM  (HRTEM, Fig. 2(c))  showed  that  the exposed  crystal  faces  of $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  were  (200)  and  (111), and  energy  dispersive  X-ray  spectroscopy  (EDS)  mapping  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ further proved that the ultrafine $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  were  highly  distributed  on  the  MOF  (Fig. 2(d)). Moreover, the EDS analysis revealed that the line scan profile for silver agreed well with those for $\mathrm{zr}$ and N, which are the main components  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ (Fig. 2(e)).  These  results  again demonstrated  that  the  Ag  nanoparticles  were  uniformly distributed in the meso-channels of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$
In  this  work,  to  investigate  the  catalytic  activities  of  the  asprepared $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst, the 4-NP reduction by $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ was evaluated as a benchmark reaction. The variation of 4-NP was measured  through  UV–vis  spectroscopy  after  the  addition  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to  the  alkaline  solution.  As  the  reaction processed, the absorption peak of 4-nitrophenolate ion at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ decreased along with the increasing new band of 4-AP at $300\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S7 in the ESM). By analyzing the nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra of the reaction products (Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM),  it  further  proved  that  the  nitro  groups  were  successfully converted  into  the  corresponding  amine  groups.  As  depicted  in Fig. 3(a) and  movies  (Movies  S1  and  S2  in  the  ESM),  the completed reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP was achieved within only 90 s  immediately  after  the  addition  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to  the reaction system $(n_{[4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}=810),$ .
In comparison, the $\mathrm{Ag/UiO-}66$ catalyst with no $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups afforded a poor catalytic performance with a complete conversion time  of  210  s $(n_{[4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}=810)$ (Fig. S10  in  the  ESM).  By applying  the  pseudo-first-order  kinetic  model,  a  good  linear correlation  between $\ln(C_{\mathrm{t}}/C_{0})$ and  reaction  time $t$ could  be obtained to evaluate the reaction rates of 4-NP reduction. Based on  the  detailed  experimental  condition,  a  TOF  (normalized  per mole  of  Ag)  as  high  as $3.2\times10^{4}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ could  be  derived,  which demonstrated  superior  catalytic  performances  as  compared  with that of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO-}66$ $(1.4\times10^{4}\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ , Fig. 3(b)). As plotted in Fig. 3(c), the $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst  exhibited  an  extraordinarily  high reaction rate $k$ of $6.9\times10^{-2}\ s^{-1}$ . In contrast, the estimated reaction rate $k$ value of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO-}66$ catalyst was only $2.2\times10^{-2}\:\mathrm{s^{-1}}.$ , implying $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has  an  enhanced  catalytic  activity.  This  result further confirmed that the efficient stabilization/immobilization of $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  could  be  achieved  via  a  strong  coordination, which is provided by the lone pair electrons of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups and empty d-orbital of $\mathrm{Ag}$ species [15].
For  a  better  understanding  of  the  catalytic  capabilities  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the  influence  of  different  catalyst  dosages  was carefully investigated and the results are illustrated in Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S11  in  the  ESM.  When  the  molar  ratios  of $n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}}/n_{\mathrm{[Ag]}}$ increased  from  540  to  810,  the  reaction  rate $k$ value  was significantly  improved  from $2.7\times10^{-2}$ to $6.9\times10^{-2}\ s^{-1}$ .  Further addition of the catalyst led to a slightly higher $k$ value $(9.0\times10^{-2}$ $\mathbf{s}^{\scriptscriptstyle-1},$ .  Subsequently,  the  influence  of  the $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ concentration  on the catalytic activity was evaluated, which exhibited a similar trend to that of catalyst dosages. Impressively, it can be found that the concentration  of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ favours  the  4-NP  reduction  with  a constant $k$ value  up  to $6.9\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ s^{-1}$ .  Notably,  increasing  the $n_{\mathrm{[NaBH4]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}}$ molar  ratio  to  higher  than  200  did  not  have  a critical effect on the catalytic performance. This was also verified by the pseudo-first-order kinetic curves (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S12 in the  ESM).  These  observations  fundamentally  implied  that  the $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ concentration was the vital factor to control the catalytic performance during the liquid-phase catalytic reactions. Accordingly,  it  can  be  concluded  that  the  optimal  reaction condition  for  4-NP  reduction  was  using $n_{\mathrm{[NaBH4]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}}$ and $n_{[4\AA]}$ $_\mathrm{NP}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}$ molar ratios of 200 and 810, respectively.
Moreover, the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ did not show the activity for the reduction  of  4-NP  under  the  light,  which  was  evidenced  by  the control  experiment  (Fig. S13  in  the  ESM).  In  addition,  the conversion of 4-NP over $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under light and dark is similar (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Currently, heterogeneous catalysts, including  CdS, ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4},}$ have  been  reported  as emerging  green  technologies  used  for  photocatalysis  reactions [35–38].  These  advanced  nanostructured  materials  show promising photocatalytic activity due to their strong quantum size effect  and  appropriate  band  positions.  However,  the  electronic conductivity of the pristine MOFs used in the present work was inadequate, indicating there was no photocatalytic activity during the hydrogenation of 4-NP to 4-AP under the reaction conditions [39].
Durability is the essential criterion for the actual application of catalysts  [40, 41].  The  longevity  of  the  developed  catalyst  was established  by  two  tests.  First,  they  were  recycled  10  times  and exhibited  undiminished  activity  (Fig. 3(f)).  The  leaching  tests indicated that no further conversion of 4-NP was observed as the solid catalyst was separated from the reaction solution by filtration (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Second, the durability of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ catalyst  under  a  long-term  microflow  reduction  condition  was examined  (Fig. 4(a)).  When  the  microflow  4-NP  reduction  was conducted at $298\mathrm{K}$ at a flow rate of $0.5~\mathrm{{mL}\cdot\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ (contact time of $\textbf{8}s\mathbf{\backslash}$ ),  the  conversion  of  4-NP  was  maintained  at $100\%$ over $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 4(b)). This result indicated that the chemical microenvironment  around  Ag  nanoparticles  could  easily modulate  the  electronic  interaction  between  Ag  species  and supports, thus enhancing the catalytic durability [42]. Subsequently, the activity of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gradually decreased, and at $^{35\mathrm{~h,~}}$ the yield eventually fell to $10\%$ . At a flow rate of 1.5 $\mathrm{{\mL}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{min}}^{-1}$ ,  a  significant  decrease  in  the  conversion  of  4-NP  was observed after $^{2\mathrm{h},}$ and at $26\mathrm{h},$ the conversion had fallen to $13\%$ . Rapid  flow,  therefore,  facilitated  the  deactivation  of  Ag/UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . In the microflow reduction at a flow rate of $2.5\mathrm{{mL}\cdot\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ , the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was almost lost after $18\mathrm{h}$ .
ICP analysis of the liquid (flow rate of $1.5~\mathrm{{mL}\cdot\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ revealed that the $\mathrm{Ag}$ content was 5.1 ppb when 4-NP conversion was $100\%$ for $2.5\mathrm{h}.$ . Remarkably, prolonging the test time to $15\mathrm{{h}}$ with a 4- NP conversion of $50\%$ resulted in almost unchanged $\mathrm{Ag}$ content of $2.1\ \mathrm{ppb},$ validating the leaching resistance and durability of the supported metal catalyst. Moreover, the valence state of $\mathrm{Ag}$ was found to be very stable during the reaction as revealed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization (Fig. S16 in the ESM).  However,  HRTEM  analysis  of  the  recycled  Ag/UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst after the long-term reduction reactions revealed the presence  of  large  aggregates  and  enlargements  of  the $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  during  the  microflow  reactions  (Fig. S17  in  the ESM). Accordingly, the size changes of the nanoparticles may be probably responsible for the deactivation of the catalyst. Anyhow, both  batch  and  flow-through  reaction  conditions  demonstrated the excellent stability and durability of $\mathrm{\dot{A}g/U i O-}66{-}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ .
To more intuitively compare our work with previous studies, we plotted the TOF according to different types of metal catalysts, and $k$ values, as well as different ratios of $n_{\mathrm{[NaBH4]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}}$ (Fig. 5 and Table  S3  in  the  ESM).  Clearly,  as  described  in Fig. 5(a),  the performance of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ for catalytic 4-NP reduction in this  present  work  displays  one  of  the  highest  TOF  and  exceeds most  of  monometallic  and  bimetallic  catalysts  (such  as $\mathrm{Ag}$ [43–48], Pd [49–51], Au [52–56], Co [57, 58], Pd-Ag [59], and PdAu  [60]).  It  has  a $k$ value  of $6.9\times10^{-2}\ s^{-1};$ ,  to  the  best  of  our knowledge,  which  is  among  the  top  of  the  state-of-the-art  (Fig. $5(\mathrm{b})\dot{}$ ).  Moreover,  our  catalytic  system  showed  a  higher  catalytic efficiency, which could be achieved with a relatively lower $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ concentration  as  compared  to  the  reported  data  (Fig. 5(c)),  thus undoubtedly reducing the cost for organic wastewater treatment. In short, the optimized $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalytic system towards
4-NP reduction exhibits the merits of superior activities (TOF 3.2 $\times~10^{4}~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ and $k$ value $6.9\times10^{-2}\ \mathbf{S}^{-1},$ , cost-effectiveness under the lowest $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ concentration $(n_{\mathrm{[NaBH4]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}},$ 200), and outstanding cyclability (10 recycling runs) as well as good longterm  durability,  outperforming  the  capacity  of  many  previously reported catalysts.
Combined  with  the  experimental  results  and  the  literature reports [17, 61], the suggested reaction mechanism of the $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}.$ - assisted  4-NP  reduction  over $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst  is schematically illustrated in Fig. 6. The catalytic reduction process of 4-NP mainly involves the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{BH}_{4}^{-}$ , the adsorption of 4- NP,  and  the  desorption  of  4-AP.  Firstly,  the  generated  active hydrogen  after $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ hydrolysis  would  be  transferred  to  the metal surface. Subsequently, 4-NP adsorbed on $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles further  reacted  with  active  hydrogen  and  was  hydrogenated  to produce 4-AP. Among them, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups provide an excellent environment  for  the  stabilization  and  immobilization  of  Ag nanoparticles  to  ensure  that $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  could  be  subtly dispersed  on  the  amine-functionalized  MOF  structures.  In addition,  the  porous  structure  of  UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ can  promote  the diffusion of substrates and products, which greatly accelerates the reaction rate. Accordingly, it can be concluded that the favorable microenvironment  modulation  of  MOF  structure  regulates  the intrinsic properties of active sites and thus improves the electrontransfer process in the $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ -assisted 4-NP reduction system.
The highly active and stable Ag nanoparticles immobilized on UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ encourage us to explore their potential for catalytic reduction of other organic contaminants in wastewater, including cationic dyes (ie., RhB, R6G, and MB) and anionic dyes (ie., CR and MO), which are well-known toxic pollutants to human health [62]. As depicted in Fig. 7, the $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ system exhibited extremely  fast  removal  rates  when  reacting  with  RhB  and  R6G, moderate rates when reacting with CR and MO, and slow reaction rates when reacting with MB. Benefiting from the broad substratereactivity and compatibility, the prepared $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ offered significant advantages as an efficient and versatile catalyst towards selective degradation of organic pollutants for water remediation under  mild  conditions.  Such  outstanding  catalytic  performance indicates that the strategy of immobilizing metal nanoparticles on MOF  paves  an  effective  way  for  developing  highly  active heterogeneous catalysts.
## 4    Conclusion
In  summary,  we  have  developed  a  facile  approach  to  fabricate $\mathrm{Ag/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ catalyst utilizing ammine-containing $\mathrm{[Ag(NH}_{3})_{2}]^{+}$ complex as a precursor to be immobilized on UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The Ag nanoparticles exhibit good dispersibility in Ag/UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ due to the stabilizing effect of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups. Impressively, the  performance  of $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits  the  merits  of superior activities (TOF $3.2\times10^{4}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and $k$ value $6.9\times10^{-2}\ {\mathsf{s}}^{-1})$ , cost-effectiveness  under  the  lowest $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ concentration $(n_{\mathrm{[NaBH4]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}},~200)$ ,  and  outstanding  cyclability  (10  recycling runs).  Further  experimental  studies  disclose  that  the  favorable microenvironment  modulation  of  unique  MOF  structure regulates the intrinsic properties of active sites and improves the electron-transfer  process.  Additionally,  the $\mathrm{Ag/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ also promotes  the  catalytic  reduction  of  several  organic  pollutants  at room temperature and hence could find a broad application in the areas of water remediation. We envision that the present study can provide  an  advanced  strategy  for  the  rational  design  of  MOFbased  catalysts  with  excellent  activity  and  durability  in  the sustainable treatment of wastewater pollutants.
